<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.8.2: Recognizing the Expanded Product of the Difference of Two Squares</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02214</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.8.2: Recognizing the Expanded Product of the Difference of Two Squares</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e81e8d2a-8bd7-454d-9c2c-60a1c3a93942</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Clare claims that \((10 + 3)(10 - 3)\) is equivalent to \(10^2-3^2\) and \((20 + 1)(20 - 1)\) is equivalent to \(20^2-1^2\). Do you agree? Show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Enter your answer and your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00004">Agree.</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\((10+3)(10-3)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;{=\left(10+3\right)\left(10+(-3)\right)\\=10^2+10\left(-3\right)+3\left(10\right)+3\left(-3\right)\\=10^2-30+30-3^2\\=10^2-3^2}\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00006">\((20 + 1)(20 - 1)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;{=\left(20 + (-1)\right)\left(20 -1\right)\\= 20^2+ 20\left(-1\right) + 20\left(1\right) + 1\left(-1\right) \\= 20^2- 12}\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>You can use your observations from the first question to mentally solve an expression such as \(105 \cdot 95\).</item>
      </list>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">First, think about the problem differently. Evaluate \((100 + 5)(100 - 5)\) using the distributive property, or FOIL. Show your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Enter your answer and your reasoning.</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">\((100 + 5)(100 - 5) = 100^2- 5^2= 10,000 - 25 = 9975\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Check your answer by computing \(105 \cdot 95\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Enter your answer.</para>
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">\(105 \cdot 95 = 9975\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">Is \((x + 4)(x - 4)\) equivalent to \(x^2-4^2\)?</para>
          <para id="para-00016">First, support your thinking using a diagram.</para>
          <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable" id="table-00001">
            <tgroup cols="3">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis/></entry>
                  <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry><emphasis> 4 </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis> -4 </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
          <para id="para-00017">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00019">Yes.</para>
          <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable" id="table-00002">
            <tgroup cols="3">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis/></entry>
                  <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry><emphasis> 4 </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> \(x^2\) </entry>
                  <entry> \(4x\) </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis> -4 </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> \(-4x\) </entry>
                  <entry> \(-4(4)\) </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00020">Now, support your thinking using the distributive property, or FOIL.</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00023">\((x + 4)(x - 4)= x^2+ 4x - 4x - 4(4) = x^2- 4^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00024">Is \((x + 4)^2\) equivalent to \(x^2+4^2\)? Support your answer, either with or without a diagram.</para>
          <para id="para-00025">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00027">No. With a diagram:</para>
          <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable" id="table-00003">
            <tgroup cols="3">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis/></entry>
                  <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry><emphasis> 4 </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> \(x^2\) </entry>
                  <entry> \(4x\) </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis> 4 </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> \(4x\) </entry>
                  <entry> \(4(4)\) </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
          <para id="para-00028">With FOIL: \((x + 4)^2= (x + 4)(x + 4) = x^2+4x +4x +4(4) = x^2+8x +16\) \(x^2+ 4^2 = x^2+ 16\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para id="para-00029">Explain how your work in the previous questions can help you mentally evaluate \(22 \cdot 18\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00030">Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00032">\(22 \cdot 18\) can be written as \((20 + 2)(20 - 2)\), which is equivalent to \(202 - 22\), which is \(400 - 4\), or 396.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00033">Explain how your work in the previous questions can help you mentally evaluate \(45 \cdot 35\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00034">Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para id="para-00035">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00036">\(45 \cdot 35\) can be written as \((40 + 5)(40 - 5)\), which is equivalent to \(40^2-5^2\), which is \(1600 - 25\), or 1575.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00037">Here is a shortcut that can be used to mentally square any two-digit number. Let's take \(83^2\), for example.</para>
          <para id="para-00038">83 is \(80 + 3\).</para>
          <para id="para-00039">Compute \(80^2\) and \(3^2\), which gives 6400 and 9. Add these values to get 6409.</para>
          <para id="para-00040">Compute \(80 \cdot 3\), which is 240. Double it to get 480.</para>
          <para id="para-00041">Add 6409 and 480 to get 6889.</para>
          <para id="para-00042">Try using this method to find the squares of some other two-digit numbers. (With some practice, it is possible to get really fast at this!)</para>
          <para id="para-00043">Explain why this method works.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00044">Enter your explanation.</para>
          <para id="para-00045">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00046">Any two-digit number can be written as \(a + b\), where \(a\) is 10 times the first digit and \(b\) is the second digit. We are interested in squaring \(a + b\):</para>
          <para id="para-00047">\((a + b)^2=(a + b)(a + b)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00048">\(= a^2+ab +ab +b^2\) by the distributive property</para>
          <para id="para-00049">\(= a^2+ 2ab +b^2\) by combining like terms</para>
          <para id="para-00050">\(= a^2+b^2+2ab\) because addition is commutative</para>
          <para id="para-00051">We get the \(a^2 + b^2\) from squaring \(a\) and \(b\) and adding them, and the \(2ab\) from multiplying the two values and doubling them.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00052">Which of the following is equivalent to \(9^2 − 6^2\)?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Recognizing the Expanded Product of the Difference of Two Squares</title>
        <para id="para-00053">Mathematically, something special happens when two binomials that are conjugates are multiplied. In other words, the product of an expression of the form (\(a - b)(a + b)\) results in a unique expression called a difference of two squares. Here are some examples of these special products.</para>
        <para id="para-00054"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00055">Let's look at the expression \((12 + 5)(12 − 5)\). We want to determine if it is equivalent to \(12^2−5^2\).</para>
        <para id="para-00056">We can use the distributive property of multiplication over addition, or FOIL process, to multiply.</para>
        <para id="para-00057">\((12 + 5)(12 − 5)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl} &amp;=&amp;12^2-12\left(5\right)+5\left(12\right)-5^2\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;12^2-60+60-5^2\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;12^2-5^2\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\) So, \((12 + 5)(12 − 5)\) is equivalent to \(12^2-5^2\).</para>
        <para id="para-00058"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00059">Let's look at a different example using a <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">variable</term>.</para>
        <para id="para-00060">\((x + 7)(x − 7)\)</para>
        <para id="para-00061">Is this equivalent to \(x^2−7^2\)?</para>
        <para id="para-00062">\((x + 7)(x − 7)\) \(\begin{array}{rcl} &amp;=&amp;x^2-7x+7x-7^2\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;x^2-7^2=x^2-49\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\) This can also be shown using a diagram.</para>
        <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable" id="table-00004">
          <tgroup cols="3">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis/></entry>
                <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry><emphasis> –7 </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> \(x^2\) </entry>
                <entry> \(-7x\) </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry><emphasis> 7 </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> \(7x\) </entry>
                <entry> \(-7(7)\) </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00063">You have studied this process earlier when you learned the difference of squares.</para>
        <para id="para-00064">In this lesson, we will reverse this process to rewrite the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">standard form</term> of a <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">quadratic expression</term> into its <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00004">factored form</term>.</para>
        <para id="para-00065">It is important to remember the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00005">linear term</term>, or middle term of a quadratic trinomial, equals zero only holds true for the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00006">difference of squares</term>. It does not work for an expression such as \(x^2+25\). (In fact, there is no way to multiply two binomials that would result in this expression.)</para>
        <para id="para-00066"><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00067">Is \((x + 5)^2\) equivalent to \(x^2+25\)?</para>
        <para id="para-00068">Let's find the equivalent expression for \((x + 5)^2\) or \((x + 5)(x + 5)\).</para>
        <para id="para-00069">\(\begin{array}{rcl}\left(x+5\right)^2&amp;=&amp;\left(x+5\right)\left(x+5\right)\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+5x+5x+25\\&amp;&amp;\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+10x+25\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\) Since \(x^2+ 10x + 25\) is not equivalent to \(x^2+25\), these expressions are not the same.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Recognizing the Expanded Product of the Difference of Two Squares</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
            <problem id="prob-00010">
              <para id="para-00070">Is the expression \((8 − 7)(8 + 7)\) equivalent to the expression \(8^2−7^2\)? Show your reasoning using the distributive property, or FOIL.</para>
              <para id="para-00071">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00010">
              <para id="para-00072">Here is how to recognize the expanded product of the difference of two squares:</para>
              <para id="para-00073">Yes. Here is how to use the difference of squares to show equivalency between the expressions:</para>
              <para id="para-00074">\((8 − 7)(8 + 7) = 8^2+ 56 − 56 − 7^2= 8^2 − 7^2\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00011">
            <problem id="prob-00011">
              <para id="para-00075">Is \((x + 4)(x + 4)\) equivalent to \(x^2+ 4^2\)? Support your answer with a diagram.</para>
              <para id="para-00076">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00011">
              <para id="para-00077">Here is how to recognize the expanded product of the difference of two squares:</para>
              <para id="para-00078">No. The expressions are not equivalent.</para>
              <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable" id="table-00005">
                <tgroup cols="3">
                  <tbody>
                    <row>
                      <entry><emphasis/></entry>
                      <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry><emphasis> 4 </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry><emphasis> \(x\) </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry> \(x^2\) </entry>
                      <entry> \(4x\) </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry><emphasis> 4 </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry> \(4x\) </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <para id="para-00079">\(4(4)\)</para>
                      </entry>
                    </row>
                  </tbody>
                </tgroup>
              </table>
              <para id="para-00080">\((x + 4)(x + 4) = x^2 + 8x + 4^2 = x^2 + 8x + 16\)</para>
              <para id="para-00081">It is not equivalent to \(x^2 + 4^2 = x^2 + 16\).</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>